1
00:00:01,335 --> 00:00:02,963
♪♪

2
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:57,705
For you, dear Ben.

3
00:02:59,513 --> 00:03:00,572
Thank you.

4
00:03:02,449 --> 00:03:04,247
Won't you come in?

5
00:03:10,724 --> 00:03:15,458
Diana, you look, uh, different.

6
00:03:18,365 --> 00:03:21,267
You mean serene? Tranquil?

7
00:03:21,401 --> 00:03:24,132
Yeah. What happened?

8
00:03:25,806 --> 00:03:29,208
I've just spent six days
at the Anona Retreat...

9
00:03:29,342 --> 00:03:32,744
Yoga, relaxation,
deep breathing.

10
00:03:34,448 --> 00:03:37,816
Anger out, tranquility in.

11
00:03:39,086 --> 00:03:42,284
Life's too short to
manifest anger, Ben.

12
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:47,154
It's just... It's just wonderful

13
00:03:47,294 --> 00:03:52,892
how sometimes a misfortune
can turn a whole life around.

14
00:03:53,033 --> 00:03:56,561
How wise you are, dear Ben.

15
00:03:56,703 --> 00:03:59,764
So how's my case coming along?

16
00:03:59,906 --> 00:04:02,137
Slow, Diana, slow.

17
00:04:02,275 --> 00:04:05,871
We don't have a shred of proof
as to who killed your husband.

18
00:04:06,012 --> 00:04:09,608
But you will, won't
you, Ben dear?

19
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:12,515
I hope so, Diana.

20
00:04:12,652 --> 00:04:19,650
I hope so, but I have to
admit right now I'm stumped.

21
00:04:19,793 --> 00:04:24,629
But I'm not paying you a small
fortune to be stumped, Ben dear.

22
00:04:24,765 --> 00:04:28,861
I'm paying you to prove that
I didn't kill that philandering...

23
00:04:29,002 --> 00:04:30,595
I'm trying my best.

24
00:04:32,739 --> 00:04:35,834
Breathe, breathe in.
Tranquil. Breathe. Tranquil.

25
00:04:35,976 --> 00:04:40,607
Don't patronize me, you...
You old... you old jackass!

26
00:04:42,883 --> 00:04:46,251
Many 30 years in the
slammer is what you need!

27
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:53,150
Randy came by when
she heard about Jack.

28
00:04:53,293 --> 00:04:56,127
Yeah, well, when Jack came
by, Caroline was in a meeting,

29
00:04:56,263 --> 00:04:59,825
but Jack just barged right in, talked
to her for, I'd say, about 30 seconds,

30
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:01,457
and then he came out again.

31
00:05:01,601 --> 00:05:03,570
But on his way out,
he made this remark

32
00:05:03,703 --> 00:05:06,434
that sounded like Caroline
was selling the clients a Warhol.

33
00:05:06,573 --> 00:05:10,203
See, Jack told me that the picture in
Caroline's office showed... get this...

34
00:05:10,343 --> 00:05:12,778
Six rows of $50 bills.

35
00:05:12,913 --> 00:05:15,576
See, Warhol painted
a huge $1.00 bill,

36
00:05:15,715 --> 00:05:18,275
but I have never heard
of him painting a lot of 50s.

37
00:05:18,418 --> 00:05:21,479
Besides that, I went ahead and checked
the sales and the purchase records.

38
00:05:21,621 --> 00:05:23,522
We haven't handled a
Warhol in over two years.

39
00:05:23,657 --> 00:05:25,956
Well, tell him
the rest. Tell him.

40
00:05:26,092 --> 00:05:29,290
Well, a few minutes after Jack left,
Caroline came out of the conference room,

41
00:05:29,429 --> 00:05:32,695
and she was really upset, and so
were the guys she was meeting with.

42
00:05:32,833 --> 00:05:34,495
Remember their names?

43
00:05:34,634 --> 00:05:37,160
Mm-hmm. Carl
Shepley and Dick Lerner.

44
00:05:37,304 --> 00:05:39,933
- Carl Shepley?
- Yeah. Do you know him?

45
00:05:41,474 --> 00:05:44,103
Those are the three people
that John Resnick called

46
00:05:44,244 --> 00:05:46,577
- the night before he was murdered.
- Hmm.

47
00:05:46,713 --> 00:05:48,891
Well, yeah, you know, they're
at the art gallery all the time.

48
00:05:48,915 --> 00:05:51,460
That's why I really couldn't
understand why Caroline got so upset.

49
00:05:51,484 --> 00:05:55,080
Ever heard of an artist
named Hugo Wykoff?

50
00:05:55,222 --> 00:05:59,284
Oh, I have. We handled
his lithographs at the gallery.

51
00:05:59,426 --> 00:06:01,793
He was killed in a
hit-and-run about a year ago.

52
00:06:01,928 --> 00:06:04,523
That's right.

53
00:06:04,664 --> 00:06:06,462
<i>♪♪</i>

54
00:06:16,276 --> 00:06:17,369
Excuse me.

55
00:06:19,279 --> 00:06:21,248
Excuse me!

56
00:06:21,381 --> 00:06:23,145
Excuse me.

57
00:06:27,554 --> 00:06:28,783
I'm looking for Lilly Wykoff.

58
00:06:28,922 --> 00:06:31,153
Oh, that's me.

59
00:06:31,291 --> 00:06:34,728
Can I ask you something?

60
00:06:34,861 --> 00:06:36,159
What do you call all this?

61
00:06:36,296 --> 00:06:39,198
This is... It's art,
that's what it is.

62
00:06:39,332 --> 00:06:40,459
It's art.

63
00:06:42,769 --> 00:06:45,898
My name is Conrad McMasters.

64
00:06:46,039 --> 00:06:47,166
I'm a private investigator.

65
00:06:47,307 --> 00:06:50,766
I wonder if I could ask you a couple
of questions about your late husband.

66
00:06:50,911 --> 00:06:52,573
What about my husband?

67
00:06:52,712 --> 00:06:54,442
I understand he was
a wonderful artist.

68
00:06:54,581 --> 00:06:56,516
He was a great artist, my Hugo.

69
00:06:56,650 --> 00:06:58,448
Here, sit down.

70
00:06:58,585 --> 00:07:02,784
Uh, did he ever tell you about
anybody named John Resnick?

71
00:07:04,557 --> 00:07:06,685
That's the man who killed him.

72
00:07:06,826 --> 00:07:08,294
Killed him?

73
00:07:08,428 --> 00:07:11,057
He commissioned Hugo
to do some work for him.

74
00:07:11,197 --> 00:07:14,326
Now, I don't know what kind
of work; Hugo wouldn't say.

75
00:07:14,467 --> 00:07:17,494
But I do know they had a
big fight on the phone one day,

76
00:07:17,637 --> 00:07:20,436
and when Hugo hung
up, he was scared.

77
00:07:20,573 --> 00:07:23,099
Two days later, he was dead.

78
00:07:23,243 --> 00:07:27,237
The police tell me John
Resnick had an airtight alibi.

79
00:07:27,380 --> 00:07:31,181
Well, I don't care. He's the
one who ran Hugo down.

80
00:07:31,318 --> 00:07:34,288
Did Hugo ever mention
anybody named Carl Shepley?

81
00:07:35,388 --> 00:07:36,651
No.

82
00:07:36,790 --> 00:07:39,521
What about Caroline
Westlake or Dick Lerner?

83
00:07:39,659 --> 00:07:41,355
Sure, he mentioned them.

84
00:07:41,494 --> 00:07:44,020
Caroline had his work
on display in her gallery,

85
00:07:44,164 --> 00:07:47,430
and Dick Lerner bought
one of his best pieces.

86
00:07:47,567 --> 00:07:51,470
That was the last thing Hugo
sold before he was killed.

87
00:07:51,604 --> 00:07:53,869
Did you happen to save
some of your husband's art?

88
00:07:54,007 --> 00:07:56,033
I saved all of it.

89
00:07:56,176 --> 00:07:57,610
Could I see it?

90
00:07:58,778 --> 00:07:59,973
Sure.

91
00:08:02,983 --> 00:08:06,920
Oh, the nudes are
all of me, by the way.

92
00:08:25,538 --> 00:08:27,803
Uh, Mr. Shepley?

93
00:08:27,941 --> 00:08:29,876
Uh, Mr. Shepley?

94
00:08:33,747 --> 00:08:36,444
Hi. I'm Ben Matlock.

95
00:08:36,583 --> 00:08:38,279
I'm glad I finally
caught up with you.

96
00:08:38,418 --> 00:08:41,183
I've got to call you back, boss.

97
00:08:42,455 --> 00:08:44,219
What's this about, Mr. Matlock?

98
00:08:44,357 --> 00:08:50,388
Well, I represent the woman who's
accused of killing Jack Huntington,

99
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:53,364
and your name keeps
popping up all over the place.

100
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:55,162
- You're kidding me.
- Yeah.

101
00:08:55,301 --> 00:09:01,366
You're friends with John Resnick,
Caroline Westlake, and Dick Lerner.

102
00:09:01,508 --> 00:09:03,067
Were you in business with them?

103
00:09:03,209 --> 00:09:05,235
Yeah. I'm a broker.

104
00:09:05,378 --> 00:09:07,347
I bring people who want
to buy things together

105
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:09,058
with the people who
want to sell them...

106
00:09:09,082 --> 00:09:12,644
Classic cars, artwork,
real estate, dental practices.

107
00:09:12,786 --> 00:09:14,266
I'm kind of a
professional middleman.

108
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:16,050
Oh. That's good.

109
00:09:16,189 --> 00:09:17,885
What's going on here?

110
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:21,085
Well, all I know so far for sure

111
00:09:21,227 --> 00:09:25,221
is that John Resnick was involved
in a murder about a year ago

112
00:09:25,365 --> 00:09:27,561
and that he called you
at 2:00 in the morning

113
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:28,940
the night before he was killed.

114
00:09:29,002 --> 00:09:33,269
He also called Caroline Westlake
and Dick Lerner that same night,

115
00:09:33,406 --> 00:09:37,639
and they are the two people
that Jack Huntington saw you with

116
00:09:37,777 --> 00:09:41,373
when he came barging into the
conference room at the gallery

117
00:09:41,514 --> 00:09:42,982
the night before
he was murdered.

118
00:09:43,116 --> 00:09:46,814
And since you seem to be at the
center of all this death and destruction,

119
00:09:46,953 --> 00:09:49,286
I was hoping you could
tell me what's going on.

120
00:09:49,422 --> 00:09:53,985
Mr. Matlock, I would like to help
you, but I don't know what to tell you.

121
00:09:54,127 --> 00:09:55,254
Maybe you could tell me

122
00:09:55,395 --> 00:09:59,264
what you and Ms. Westlake and
Mr. Lerner were looking at that night.

123
00:09:59,399 --> 00:10:00,423
What night?

124
00:10:00,567 --> 00:10:03,696
The night Jack Huntington come
bursting into that conference room.

125
00:10:03,837 --> 00:10:06,773
Now, that was business, Mr. Matlock.
I'd just as soon not discuss that.

126
00:10:06,906 --> 00:10:09,239
How about the next
morning around, oh, say, 8:00,

127
00:10:09,375 --> 00:10:11,844
when Jack Huntington was killed?

128
00:10:11,978 --> 00:10:14,243
Could we discuss
that, or is that business?

129
00:10:16,449 --> 00:10:19,715
If memory serves, I
was playing racquetball.

130
00:10:19,853 --> 00:10:22,755
Now, if you'll excuse me,
Mr. Matlock, I have places to go.

131
00:10:25,058 --> 00:10:26,720
Uh, one more question...

132
00:10:26,860 --> 00:10:29,022
The Hillsdale Racquet Club.

133
00:10:51,518 --> 00:10:54,420
He knows about John Resnick?
My God, what else does he know?

134
00:10:54,554 --> 00:10:56,079
- Nothing.
- What if he followed you?

135
00:10:56,222 --> 00:10:58,088
- He didn't.
- Are you sure?

136
00:10:58,224 --> 00:11:00,469
Hey, I ought to know if the
guy I've been tailing for two days

137
00:11:00,493 --> 00:11:01,973
has been tailing
me, don't you think?

138
00:11:02,028 --> 00:11:04,020
So we're going to
go through with it?

139
00:11:04,164 --> 00:11:06,030
You'd better believe we are.

140
00:11:07,967 --> 00:11:12,905
Within 24 hours, the three of us
are going to get an awful lot of money.

141
00:11:24,450 --> 00:11:26,612
See, Hugo dated his lithographs.

142
00:11:26,753 --> 00:11:29,154
This is what he was
working on when he died.

143
00:11:29,289 --> 00:11:32,225
The man knew how to
draw money, didn't he?

144
00:11:32,358 --> 00:11:34,418
Right down to the
whiskers on Grant's face.

145
00:11:34,561 --> 00:11:35,585
Yeah.

146
00:11:36,963 --> 00:11:39,262
Jack saw something
he shouldn't have seen,

147
00:11:39,399 --> 00:11:42,858
and Caroline and Carl Shepley
and Dick Lerner were afraid

148
00:11:43,002 --> 00:11:44,937
he might figure out what it was,

149
00:11:45,071 --> 00:11:46,869
and so they simply killed him.

150
00:11:54,881 --> 00:11:55,940
Good morning, Randy.

151
00:11:56,082 --> 00:11:57,744
Oh, hi, Mr. Shepley.

152
00:11:57,884 --> 00:11:59,644
She's waiting for you
in the conference room.

153
00:11:59,686 --> 00:12:00,686
Great.

154
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:03,085
Don't forget the
baseboards, okay?

155
00:12:03,223 --> 00:12:04,919
- Right.
- Thanks.

156
00:12:14,834 --> 00:12:17,429
Caroline, what the
hell is wrong with you?

157
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:18,731
What's he doing here?

158
00:12:18,871 --> 00:12:19,871
- Who?
- Shh.

159
00:12:19,906 --> 00:12:21,704
The guy in the coveralls.

160
00:12:21,841 --> 00:12:23,434
He's a janitor. He's cleaning.

161
00:12:23,576 --> 00:12:26,239
The hell he is. That's Ben
Matlock's private investigator.

162
00:12:26,379 --> 00:12:28,405
I saw him outside his house.

163
00:12:44,998 --> 00:12:47,229
All right, I'm going to
pick the truck up at 3:00,

164
00:12:47,367 --> 00:12:50,132
and I should be able to
make delivery by 4:30.

165
00:12:50,270 --> 00:12:53,365
So by 7:00 it will all be over.

166
00:12:53,506 --> 00:12:54,633
Yeah.

167
00:12:58,678 --> 00:13:01,944
So the poor guy started to
run up on the grass to get away,

168
00:13:02,081 --> 00:13:04,607
but the car went right after
him and just nailed him.

169
00:13:04,751 --> 00:13:07,619
About how far away from the
decedent were you when this happened?

170
00:13:07,754 --> 00:13:09,052
Oh, 30-40 feet.

171
00:13:09,188 --> 00:13:11,316
Were you close enough to
see the car's license plate?

172
00:13:11,457 --> 00:13:12,457
Oh, yes, easily.

173
00:13:12,558 --> 00:13:14,117
- Do you remember it?
- You bet.

174
00:13:14,260 --> 00:13:18,129
It was ESV 57I.

175
00:13:18,264 --> 00:13:19,630
Georgia plates.

176
00:13:19,766 --> 00:13:22,964
Oh, and there was a frame around
the plate saying "Born to Shop."

177
00:13:24,470 --> 00:13:26,371
Did you happen to
notice the make of the car?

178
00:13:26,506 --> 00:13:29,874
Yeah. It was a blue
Lincoln Mark VII.

179
00:13:30,009 --> 00:13:33,411
Since it's been stipulated that
the defendant, Diana Huntington,

180
00:13:33,546 --> 00:13:38,610
owns a blue Lincoln Mark VII,
license plate number ESV 571,

181
00:13:38,751 --> 00:13:40,617
I have no further questions.

182
00:13:42,221 --> 00:13:44,656
- Mr. Matlock?
- Yes, sir.

183
00:13:44,791 --> 00:13:50,253
Uh, this car that
drove past you,

184
00:13:50,396 --> 00:13:53,491
did you get a
look at the driver?

185
00:13:53,633 --> 00:13:56,262
Well, no, not a
real good look, no.

186
00:13:56,402 --> 00:14:00,237
So the only view you had of the
car and its driver was from behind?

187
00:14:00,373 --> 00:14:01,373
Right.

188
00:14:01,407 --> 00:14:04,104
From behind, what
did the driver look like?

189
00:14:04,243 --> 00:14:09,045
Well, I was so busy memorizing the plate
that I didn't look at the driver at all.

190
00:14:09,182 --> 00:14:13,711
Oh, so you saw Diana Huntington's
car, but you didn't see who was driving it?

191
00:14:13,853 --> 00:14:14,853
Right.

192
00:14:14,887 --> 00:14:16,719
It could have been anybody.

193
00:14:16,856 --> 00:14:18,757
Yes, absolutely.

194
00:14:18,891 --> 00:14:21,019
Thank you.

195
00:14:44,150 --> 00:14:46,676
Miss Sullivan, Jack
Huntington was living with you

196
00:14:46,819 --> 00:14:48,263
at the time of his
death, is that correct?

197
00:14:48,287 --> 00:14:49,287
Yes.

198
00:14:49,355 --> 00:14:51,221
He had recently
separated from his wife?

199
00:14:51,357 --> 00:14:52,357
That's right.

200
00:14:52,458 --> 00:14:56,156
Wouldn't it be more accurate to say
that he was hiding from Diana Huntington?

201
00:14:56,295 --> 00:14:58,764
I couldn't really say.

202
00:14:58,898 --> 00:15:01,299
Well, didn't Jack Huntington
give specific instructions

203
00:15:01,434 --> 00:15:03,562
to both his attorney
and his accountant

204
00:15:03,703 --> 00:15:05,569
to withhold his whereabouts
from the defendant?

205
00:15:05,705 --> 00:15:06,968
Yes.

206
00:15:07,106 --> 00:15:09,302
He was afraid of his wife's
violent temper, wasn't he?

207
00:15:09,442 --> 00:15:11,934
Objection. Calls
for speculation.

208
00:15:12,078 --> 00:15:13,444
Sustained.

209
00:15:14,914 --> 00:15:19,284
Did Diana Huntington send a birthday
card to her husband through her attorney?

210
00:15:19,419 --> 00:15:20,546
Yes.

211
00:15:20,686 --> 00:15:22,245
Did you happen to see that card?

212
00:15:22,388 --> 00:15:23,651
Oh, I sure did.

213
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:25,759
And what did it say?

214
00:15:26,926 --> 00:15:29,919
It said, "Happy birthday,
dear. Wish you were dead."

215
00:15:30,062 --> 00:15:31,428
That was a joke!

216
00:15:31,564 --> 00:15:32,930
It was a joke!

217
00:15:33,065 --> 00:15:35,466
Order. Mr. Matlock,
restrain your client.

218
00:15:35,601 --> 00:15:37,126
Sit down, Diana.

219
00:15:37,270 --> 00:15:40,399
It was a joke. Aren't you
going to say something?

220
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:42,270
Miss Sullivan,

221
00:15:42,408 --> 00:15:47,073
did the defendant recently visit you
and Jack Huntington at your home?

222
00:15:47,213 --> 00:15:48,272
Yes.

223
00:15:48,414 --> 00:15:53,079
How would you characterize her state
of mind when she entered your house?

224
00:15:53,219 --> 00:15:56,656
She... She was upset.

225
00:15:56,789 --> 00:15:58,553
Upset?

226
00:15:58,691 --> 00:16:02,890
Didn't she burst through
your front door flailing a gun?

227
00:16:03,029 --> 00:16:06,022
That gun wasn't
loaded, you cretin.

228
00:16:06,165 --> 00:16:08,361
Would you get off your
duff and say something?

229
00:16:08,501 --> 00:16:11,499
Diana... Quiet. I'll
hold you in contempt.

230
00:16:11,637 --> 00:16:13,162
Do you call this justice?

231
00:16:13,306 --> 00:16:14,330
Quiet!

232
00:16:29,789 --> 00:16:31,724
Oh, God, no.

233
00:16:31,858 --> 00:16:33,190
No. Please don't.

234
00:16:33,326 --> 00:16:35,420
Um, I'm not armed.

235
00:16:35,561 --> 00:16:39,157
I had no control over the
prosecutor's questions today, Diana.

236
00:16:39,298 --> 00:16:40,298
I know that.

237
00:16:40,433 --> 00:16:41,633
I just answered them honestly.

238
00:16:41,667 --> 00:16:43,135
I know that, too, Lisa.

239
00:16:43,269 --> 00:16:45,761
I don't want to talk about
what happened in court today.

240
00:16:45,905 --> 00:16:47,865
I just want to ask you
something. Could I come in?

241
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:04,355
Mm. So this is how artists live.

242
00:17:04,490 --> 00:17:06,584
I'm comfortable with it.

243
00:17:06,726 --> 00:17:08,456
So was Jack.

244
00:17:11,163 --> 00:17:13,530
Oh, my gosh.

245
00:17:14,767 --> 00:17:21,401
Did you know that I knit this for
Jack with my own hands 15 years ago?

246
00:17:24,377 --> 00:17:27,711
I'm glad to see he held
on to something of mine.

247
00:17:28,848 --> 00:17:30,339
Do you knit?

248
00:17:30,483 --> 00:17:32,145
No. My mother does.

249
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:37,688
Diana, you wanted to talk
to me about something?

250
00:17:37,823 --> 00:17:39,189
Yes.

251
00:17:40,393 --> 00:17:43,852
I don't know why this is
important to me, but it is.

252
00:17:43,996 --> 00:17:45,658
Were you going to marry Jack?

253
00:17:45,798 --> 00:17:47,790
That's your question?

254
00:17:47,934 --> 00:17:50,733
I don't know. We
never talked about it.

255
00:17:50,870 --> 00:17:52,361
Oh, come on.

256
00:17:52,505 --> 00:17:55,907
Two people who are presumably in
love talk to each other about their future.

257
00:17:56,042 --> 00:17:58,273
Yeah, we did, just
not in those terms.

258
00:17:58,411 --> 00:17:59,640
I don't know what you mean.

259
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:04,710
Getting married
wasn't important to me.

260
00:18:04,850 --> 00:18:07,760
I just wanted to be
with Jack, that's all.

261
00:18:07,784 --> 00:18:08,184
Huh.

262
00:18:08,321 --> 00:18:11,018
Marriage was important
to your generation.

263
00:18:12,124 --> 00:18:14,389
My generation?

264
00:18:14,527 --> 00:18:15,961
Look, Diana, I
don't want to fight.

265
00:18:16,095 --> 00:18:18,360
You and me, we're different.

266
00:18:18,497 --> 00:18:21,092
Oh, we sure are.

267
00:18:25,905 --> 00:18:27,931
No, this is wrong.

268
00:18:29,609 --> 00:18:32,044
I loved Jack, and I
guess you did, too.

269
00:18:32,178 --> 00:18:37,082
In our very different
way, we'll both miss him.

270
00:18:44,423 --> 00:18:47,825
<i>Trevor Bone, bring up
number 16. It's the white van.</i>

271
00:20:03,135 --> 00:20:05,195
The coast couldn't be clearer.

272
00:21:43,435 --> 00:21:45,063
Where's Charles Beckman?

273
00:22:00,219 --> 00:22:01,687
A million.

274
00:22:03,122 --> 00:22:05,216
Now we see what you've got.

275
00:22:18,103 --> 00:22:19,799
Ten million in 50s.

276
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:22,103
Hold it.

277
00:22:22,241 --> 00:22:23,675
This one.

278
00:22:38,724 --> 00:22:39,953
Okay, Charlie.

279
00:22:48,234 --> 00:22:50,931
One thousand percent
return on your money.

280
00:22:51,070 --> 00:22:52,663
You can't beat that.

281
00:22:52,805 --> 00:22:55,707
Beats the hell out of a
two-year CD, that's for sure.

282
00:22:55,841 --> 00:22:57,503
I'll be in touch.

283
00:23:09,088 --> 00:23:11,057
Hold it right there!
Nobody move!

284
00:24:16,355 --> 00:24:18,153
We just got done
searching the room

285
00:24:18,290 --> 00:24:20,521
you leased in that
industrial park, Mr. Lerner.

286
00:24:20,659 --> 00:24:22,389
We had a warrant,
of course, counselor.

287
00:24:22,528 --> 00:24:25,123
We found a printing
press and two plates...

288
00:24:25,264 --> 00:24:28,359
One for the front of a $50
bill and one for the back.

289
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:30,059
Who made the plates for you?

290
00:24:30,202 --> 00:24:31,864
My clients have nothing to say.

291
00:24:32,004 --> 00:24:34,098
Was it Hugo Wykoff?

292
00:24:34,239 --> 00:24:39,542
We also have the whole transaction between
you and Charles Beckman on videotape.

293
00:24:39,678 --> 00:24:41,438
In other words, we've
got you dead to rights.

294
00:24:41,513 --> 00:24:45,075
So why don't you save the
taxpayers of this state a lot of money

295
00:24:45,217 --> 00:24:48,278
and maybe yourselves some prison
time and fill us in on all the details?

296
00:24:48,420 --> 00:24:50,912
I told you, my clients have
nothing to say, Lieutenant.

297
00:24:51,056 --> 00:24:54,049
Well, well, Mr. Shepley.

298
00:24:54,193 --> 00:24:55,217
Pull up a chair.

299
00:24:55,361 --> 00:24:57,330
Glad you could join us.

300
00:24:58,530 --> 00:25:00,761
I take it you've been
advised of your rights?

301
00:25:00,899 --> 00:25:02,162
My lawyer's on his way.

302
00:25:02,301 --> 00:25:05,430
We've been sitting here
discussing this counterfeiting ring...

303
00:25:05,571 --> 00:25:08,666
You mean you were just
discussing this alleged ring.

304
00:25:08,807 --> 00:25:10,151
The three of you
are involved with.

305
00:25:10,175 --> 00:25:11,820
If you'll recall, my
clients have said nothing.

306
00:25:11,844 --> 00:25:13,164
Well, they'd better
say something

307
00:25:13,278 --> 00:25:16,442
because we're not just
talking about counterfeit here.

308
00:25:16,582 --> 00:25:20,110
We're talking about murder,
and that's a capital offense.

309
00:25:20,252 --> 00:25:23,552
And one or all of you could
be spending some time...

310
00:25:23,689 --> 00:25:27,023
And I'm not talking about
some federal country club.

311
00:25:27,159 --> 00:25:28,525
I'm talking about hard time.

312
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:30,128
State prison.

313
00:25:30,262 --> 00:25:32,663
And I'm not talking
about two or three years.

314
00:25:32,798 --> 00:25:34,960
I'm talking about
the rest of your lives.

315
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:37,592
My clients have nothing to
say on that subject, either.

316
00:25:37,736 --> 00:25:38,795
The hell they don't.

317
00:25:38,937 --> 00:25:40,177
When I started asking questions

318
00:25:40,239 --> 00:25:42,674
about that picture you
dummied up for John Resnick,

319
00:25:42,808 --> 00:25:45,243
you figured you'd better
keep an eye on me, didn't you?

320
00:25:45,377 --> 00:25:47,141
That's how you got onto Conrad.

321
00:25:47,279 --> 00:25:51,046
And that's how you knew Diana
Huntington would be a perfect patsy for you

322
00:25:51,183 --> 00:25:53,516
when you realized you
had to kill her husband.

323
00:25:53,652 --> 00:25:55,052
I insist you stop
this badgering.

324
00:25:55,187 --> 00:25:57,679
One of you stole her
car and ran him down.

325
00:25:57,823 --> 00:26:00,019
I'm as sure of that
as I'm sitting here.

326
00:26:00,159 --> 00:26:01,752
I just don't know
which one... you?

327
00:26:01,894 --> 00:26:04,489
I told you, I was playing
racquetball that morning.

328
00:26:04,630 --> 00:26:06,189
- You?
- Don't answer that.

329
00:26:06,331 --> 00:26:09,130
I was at Chez Sal's
making a sales pitch.

330
00:26:09,268 --> 00:26:11,567
You, where were you
at 8:00 that morning?

331
00:26:11,703 --> 00:26:14,764
You don't have to say anything.

332
00:26:14,907 --> 00:26:16,273
I was in bed.

333
00:26:16,408 --> 00:26:18,673
- Alone?
- Don't answer that!

334
00:26:18,811 --> 00:26:20,473
Yes.

335
00:26:21,480 --> 00:26:23,676
So you can't prove
you were in bed.

336
00:26:26,185 --> 00:26:29,019
And you can't prove I wasn't.

337
00:26:45,070 --> 00:26:47,562
Diana, what are you doing here?

338
00:26:47,706 --> 00:26:48,799
I brought her.

339
00:26:48,941 --> 00:26:51,069
You went and told her
I was making progress.

340
00:26:51,210 --> 00:26:52,838
She followed me
following Shepley.

341
00:26:52,978 --> 00:26:54,344
She blew my cover.

342
00:26:54,480 --> 00:26:56,073
No, I didn't.

343
00:26:56,215 --> 00:26:59,617
What do you call it when the person
you're trying to secretly watch sees you?

344
00:26:59,751 --> 00:27:03,119
Well, I didn't know you had a
cover, so how could I blow it?

345
00:27:03,255 --> 00:27:06,020
Diana, you shouldn't
have followed Conrad.

346
00:27:06,158 --> 00:27:08,127
Oh, please, don't yell at me.

347
00:27:08,260 --> 00:27:10,491
Oh, since when did
you mind yelling?

348
00:27:10,629 --> 00:27:14,293
Well, you know Dr. Dennis
Woodruff, the radio therapist?

349
00:27:14,433 --> 00:27:16,595
I called him yesterday,
and I found out

350
00:27:16,735 --> 00:27:20,172
that my anger is just a cover
for my extreme sensitivity.

351
00:27:23,942 --> 00:27:28,175
Diana, you need therapy, yeah,

352
00:27:28,313 --> 00:27:31,442
but not out of books
or over the radio.

353
00:27:31,583 --> 00:27:36,283
You need a bona fide doctor.

354
00:27:36,421 --> 00:27:38,390
Somebody you can interact with.

355
00:27:38,524 --> 00:27:40,083
You think so?

356
00:27:40,225 --> 00:27:43,457
Oh, yes. Somebody
who can talk sense to you.

357
00:27:43,595 --> 00:27:46,588
Promise me you'll get
a psychiatrist, Diana.

358
00:27:46,732 --> 00:27:48,030
Okay.

359
00:27:48,167 --> 00:27:52,764
And promise me this is the last time
you'll interfere with this investigation.

360
00:27:52,905 --> 00:27:56,034
Interfere? I'm paying
you a fortune for this.

361
00:27:56,175 --> 00:27:57,575
You're working for me.

362
00:27:57,709 --> 00:28:01,407
Diana, do you want
me to stay on this case?

363
00:28:01,547 --> 00:28:03,607
Yes, dear Ben, of course I do.

364
00:28:03,749 --> 00:28:06,116
Then you've got to
stay away from here.

365
00:28:06,251 --> 00:28:07,913
Get a psychiatrist.

366
00:28:08,053 --> 00:28:11,512
Go see him as soon as you
get out of bed in the morning

367
00:28:11,657 --> 00:28:13,785
and stay there
all day, every day.

368
00:28:13,926 --> 00:28:16,725
Now get out of here.

369
00:28:21,166 --> 00:28:22,566
I'm not going to say anything.

370
00:28:45,490 --> 00:28:48,654
I just got to Caroline
Westlake's house, Ben.

371
00:28:48,794 --> 00:28:50,990
Right. I'll let you
know what I find.

372
00:29:17,823 --> 00:29:18,916
Hi.

373
00:29:19,057 --> 00:29:22,152
Hey, are you the guy that usually
reads the meters at this house?

374
00:29:22,294 --> 00:29:23,294
Mm-hmm.

375
00:29:23,362 --> 00:29:25,907
If you've got a problem with your
bill, call the folks downtown, okay?

376
00:29:25,931 --> 00:29:27,971
No, no. I just want to know
how often you come here.

377
00:29:28,066 --> 00:29:29,159
Once a month.

378
00:29:29,301 --> 00:29:30,929
- Always on Wednesdays?
- Yeah.

379
00:29:31,069 --> 00:29:32,697
- Always at 8 in the morning?
- Yeah.

380
00:29:32,838 --> 00:29:36,104
Great. By any chance, were you
here on the 18th of last month?

381
00:29:41,246 --> 00:29:44,045
Nope. I was here on the 25th.

382
00:29:44,182 --> 00:29:46,617
Got to get out of here
before the gardener comes.

383
00:29:46,752 --> 00:29:50,052
He always gives me hell when I
step on his petunias or gardenias

384
00:29:50,188 --> 00:29:52,054
or whatever the
hell this stuff is.

385
00:30:00,732 --> 00:30:03,327
Les? Hey, Les?

386
00:30:03,468 --> 00:30:04,834
What?

387
00:30:04,970 --> 00:30:06,700
Where have you been?

388
00:30:10,208 --> 00:30:11,437
When's the big day?

389
00:30:11,576 --> 00:30:13,408
There's not going
to be a big day.

390
00:30:13,545 --> 00:30:15,741
- What happened?
- I don't want to talk about it.

391
00:30:15,881 --> 00:30:17,941
Well, who called it off?

392
00:30:18,083 --> 00:30:20,484
Mildred, the bride-to-be,
that's who called it off.

393
00:30:20,619 --> 00:30:21,882
Why?

394
00:30:22,020 --> 00:30:23,545
I don't want to talk about it.

395
00:30:23,689 --> 00:30:26,124
Oh, Les, tell me what happened.

396
00:30:27,259 --> 00:30:28,259
Okay.

397
00:30:28,327 --> 00:30:31,695
She said if we got married,
she'd lose her Social Security,

398
00:30:31,830 --> 00:30:36,029
so why don't we just be friends and
just go on meeting at the supermarket.

399
00:30:36,168 --> 00:30:39,434
Oh, I'm sorry to hear that.

400
00:30:39,571 --> 00:30:41,597
Just go on meeting
at the supermarket.

401
00:30:41,740 --> 00:30:43,606
What kind of life is that?

402
00:30:43,742 --> 00:30:45,335
Yeah.

403
00:30:45,477 --> 00:30:48,777
Of course, maybe
it's for the best.

404
00:30:48,914 --> 00:30:52,248
I mean, she needs her Social
Security, and you need yours,

405
00:30:52,384 --> 00:30:55,752
so it could be a
blessing in disguise.

406
00:30:56,755 --> 00:30:58,280
Well, it could be.

407
00:30:58,423 --> 00:30:59,891
Yeah. Sure.

408
00:31:00,025 --> 00:31:04,360
And you can always keep on
helping her pick out her Casabas.

409
00:31:04,496 --> 00:31:06,055
Well, yeah, there's that.

410
00:31:06,198 --> 00:31:07,222
Sure.

411
00:31:13,405 --> 00:31:17,706
I understand you were
arrested recently, Ms. Westlake.

412
00:31:17,843 --> 00:31:20,574
Since it's already a
matter of public record,

413
00:31:20,712 --> 00:31:22,872
would you mind telling the
court what the charges were?

414
00:31:22,981 --> 00:31:26,577
On the advice of legal counsel and
in view of my Fifth Amendment rights,

415
00:31:26,718 --> 00:31:28,118
I decline to answer
that question.

416
00:31:28,253 --> 00:31:33,920
Then at this time, I would
like to enter into evidence

417
00:31:34,059 --> 00:31:37,461
this arrest report
that was filed

418
00:31:37,596 --> 00:31:42,557
at the time Ms. Westlake
was taken into custody.

419
00:31:42,701 --> 00:31:48,299
For the record, it charges
the witness, Caroline Westlake,

420
00:31:48,440 --> 00:31:53,606
with the creation and distribution
of counterfeit U.S. currency...

421
00:31:53,745 --> 00:31:56,340
Specifically,
counterfeit $50 bills.

422
00:31:56,481 --> 00:31:58,006
Is that what it
says, Ms. Westlake?

423
00:31:58,150 --> 00:31:59,311
I suppose.

424
00:31:59,451 --> 00:32:04,754
I understand that Jack
Huntington, the night before he died,

425
00:32:04,890 --> 00:32:08,418
walked in unannounced on
a meeting you were having

426
00:32:08,560 --> 00:32:10,426
in your conference
room at your art gallery.

427
00:32:10,562 --> 00:32:12,656
I'm afraid I don't recall.

428
00:32:12,798 --> 00:32:17,498
It was the meeting with
Carl Shepley and Dick Lerner,

429
00:32:17,636 --> 00:32:21,164
the one where you were
showing them that Andy Warhol.

430
00:32:21,306 --> 00:32:23,332
I don't remember
any meeting like that.

431
00:32:24,409 --> 00:32:26,173
Well, your assistant does.

432
00:32:26,311 --> 00:32:29,748
Now, I can put her on
the stand, but if I do,

433
00:32:29,881 --> 00:32:32,510
then the good men
and women of the jury

434
00:32:32,651 --> 00:32:34,677
might get the idea that
you're hiding something.

435
00:32:34,820 --> 00:32:36,413
- Objection.
- Withdrawn.

436
00:32:39,558 --> 00:32:42,255
Are you sure you don't
remember that meeting?

437
00:32:43,261 --> 00:32:46,197
- I guess. Vaguely.
- Vaguely. Okay.

438
00:32:46,331 --> 00:32:51,998
Where Carl Shepley
and Dick Lerner present?

439
00:32:52,137 --> 00:32:54,971
- I think so, yes.
- Mm-hmm.

440
00:32:55,106 --> 00:33:00,636
They were arrested for counterfeiting
at the same time you were, weren't they?

441
00:33:02,614 --> 00:33:04,105
It's public record.

442
00:33:04,249 --> 00:33:06,150
Then I guess they were.

443
00:33:06,284 --> 00:33:09,618
What were you discussing
when Jack walked in on you?

444
00:33:09,754 --> 00:33:12,223
Business. I don't
recall specifics.

445
00:33:12,357 --> 00:33:15,555
Maybe I can help you out.

446
00:33:15,694 --> 00:33:20,723
You were showing your
two friends that Andy Warhol.

447
00:33:20,866 --> 00:33:22,425
I wasn't showing
them any Warhol.

448
00:33:22,567 --> 00:33:28,529
Well, according to your assistant
Randy, Jack told her you were.

449
00:33:28,673 --> 00:33:29,902
He was mistaken.

450
00:33:30,041 --> 00:33:31,805
And according to
Jack's girlfriend,

451
00:33:31,943 --> 00:33:37,473
he said the Warhol he
saw was a sheet of $50 bills.

452
00:33:37,616 --> 00:33:41,644
- That is not true.
- Isn't that some coincidence, though?

453
00:33:41,786 --> 00:33:46,850
I mean, you're showing your
two friends a sheet of 50s,

454
00:33:46,992 --> 00:33:51,555
and a week later, you and these
same two friends are arrested

455
00:33:51,696 --> 00:33:55,929
with a truckload
of counterfeit 50s.

456
00:33:56,067 --> 00:33:58,559
I'd say that's a big
coincidence, wouldn't you?

457
00:33:58,703 --> 00:34:00,137
I choose not to comment on that.

458
00:34:03,275 --> 00:34:05,608
He saw a proof sheet.

459
00:34:06,678 --> 00:34:07,839
A proof sheet.

460
00:34:07,979 --> 00:34:12,917
It was your first run
of 50s off your press.

461
00:34:13,051 --> 00:34:19,890
He saw it, you had to explain what it
was, so you told him it was a Warhol.

462
00:34:20,025 --> 00:34:23,462
But you knew that sooner or
later he'd put 2 and 2 together,

463
00:34:23,595 --> 00:34:27,225
so you stole Diana Huntington's
car and ran Jack down in the street.

464
00:34:27,365 --> 00:34:28,731
- That's a lie!
- Objection!

465
00:34:28,867 --> 00:34:30,233
That's enough, Mr. Matlock.

466
00:34:30,368 --> 00:34:34,066
You're supposed to be questioning
this witness, not testifying in her place.

467
00:34:34,205 --> 00:34:37,175
Objection sustained.

468
00:34:37,309 --> 00:34:41,246
I'll say this just
once: cut it out!

469
00:34:41,379 --> 00:34:42,540
Yes, sir.

470
00:34:44,616 --> 00:34:50,078
Uh, at, uh, at 8:00

471
00:34:50,221 --> 00:34:55,285
the morning of Wednesday
the 18th, last month,

472
00:34:55,427 --> 00:34:58,158
the morning Jack Huntington
was killed, where were you?

473
00:34:58,296 --> 00:34:59,296
I was in bed.

474
00:34:59,397 --> 00:35:01,127
Do you always
sleep in that late?

475
00:35:01,266 --> 00:35:02,495
I'm a night owl.

476
00:35:02,634 --> 00:35:07,197
I rarely go to bed before 1:30 and
even more rarely get up before 9.

477
00:35:07,339 --> 00:35:09,501
That's what Louis said.

478
00:35:09,641 --> 00:35:13,806
Your gardener, Louis Sattoria.
He's sitting right over there.

479
00:35:13,945 --> 00:35:16,414
When he started
taking care of your lawn,

480
00:35:16,548 --> 00:35:20,918
you told him not to do any mowing
till after 9:00 so as not to wake you.

481
00:35:21,052 --> 00:35:22,452
- Is that right?
- Yes.

482
00:35:22,587 --> 00:35:23,885
Does he come every week?

483
00:35:24,022 --> 00:35:25,022
Yes.

484
00:35:25,090 --> 00:35:26,558
- What day?
- Wednesday.

485
00:35:26,691 --> 00:35:27,691
What time?

486
00:35:27,792 --> 00:35:29,055
Around 8, I guess.

487
00:35:29,194 --> 00:35:31,493
I can't say for sure
because I'm never up.

488
00:35:33,131 --> 00:35:35,657
That's not quite true, is it?

489
00:35:35,800 --> 00:35:41,239
Uh, he remembers seeing you driving
away from your house one morning

490
00:35:41,373 --> 00:35:43,103
as he was just
coming up the street.

491
00:35:43,241 --> 00:35:47,702
It stuck in his mind because he had
never seen you up so early before.

492
00:35:47,846 --> 00:35:50,042
It was 7:55.

493
00:35:50,181 --> 00:35:51,774
You know what the date was?

494
00:35:51,916 --> 00:35:55,978
Wednesday the 18th.

495
00:35:56,121 --> 00:35:58,056
Now, you said you were in bed.

496
00:35:58,189 --> 00:35:59,953
Why did you lie?

497
00:36:00,091 --> 00:36:01,457
I didn't lie. I just forgot.

498
00:36:01,593 --> 00:36:02,925
Forgot what?

499
00:36:03,061 --> 00:36:05,257
That I had a
meeting that morning.

500
00:36:05,397 --> 00:36:07,298
With whom?

501
00:36:15,173 --> 00:36:16,732
Jeffrey Aiello.

502
00:36:16,875 --> 00:36:19,003
Who's Jeffrey Aiello?

503
00:36:19,144 --> 00:36:21,613
A businessman. I was
with him till almost 9.

504
00:36:21,746 --> 00:36:23,112
What was the meeting about?

505
00:36:23,248 --> 00:36:25,877
You're going to
have to ask him that.

506
00:36:26,017 --> 00:36:28,509
Why didn't you
tell me this before?

507
00:36:28,653 --> 00:36:33,648
555-1057. That's
Aiello's number.

508
00:36:33,792 --> 00:36:37,092
When you call him,
you'll understand.

509
00:36:41,232 --> 00:36:43,167
No more questions.

510
00:36:50,742 --> 00:36:51,742
Wait up, wait up.

511
00:36:51,843 --> 00:36:54,745
I want you to find out
who Jeffrey Aiello is.

512
00:36:54,879 --> 00:36:56,370
I know him. He's a two-bit hood.

513
00:36:56,514 --> 00:36:57,709
Caroline!

514
00:36:57,849 --> 00:37:00,250
- What are you doing?
- I want...

515
00:37:00,385 --> 00:37:01,546
Get your hands off of me.

516
00:37:01,686 --> 00:37:04,622
I mean it. Stop it. Let go.

517
00:37:04,756 --> 00:37:06,190
Let go of me.

518
00:37:35,620 --> 00:37:37,521
Thanks for coming, Mr. Powell.

519
00:37:37,655 --> 00:37:39,335
Matt, do you want to
get Mr. Powell's car?

520
00:37:39,424 --> 00:37:41,154
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

521
00:37:41,292 --> 00:37:43,557
Whoa, talk about a quick
eater. Not good for you.

522
00:37:43,695 --> 00:37:46,173
- Didn't really come to eat.
- Want to get the gentlemen's car?

523
00:37:46,197 --> 00:37:47,631
- Yes, sir.
- Thank you.

524
00:37:47,766 --> 00:37:49,462
Good morning, Mrs.
Holden, Mrs. Roberts.

525
00:37:49,601 --> 00:37:51,570
- Another power breakfast, I see.
- Yes.

526
00:37:53,171 --> 00:37:54,696
Do you know everybody
that comes here?

527
00:37:54,839 --> 00:37:58,298
Most of the regulars I do. It pays
to be friendly in my line of work.

528
00:37:58,443 --> 00:38:00,139
Is Dick Lerner a regular?

529
00:38:00,278 --> 00:38:01,678
Yeah, yeah. Do you
know Mr. Lerner?

530
00:38:01,746 --> 00:38:03,715
- Sort of.
- He comes in every couple of weeks.

531
00:38:03,848 --> 00:38:06,227
Usually has eggs benedict and
leaves with a little bit on his shirt.

532
00:38:06,251 --> 00:38:09,983
That hostess in there said he was here
last month on the morning of the 18th,

533
00:38:10,121 --> 00:38:12,181
but she can't remember
exactly when he got here.

534
00:38:12,323 --> 00:38:14,155
I don't suppose you
have any idea, do you?

535
00:38:14,292 --> 00:38:16,124
No, I wouldn't remember that.

536
00:38:16,261 --> 00:38:18,061
- Why? What's this all about?
- He's a client.

537
00:38:18,129 --> 00:38:21,065
I need to corroborate whether
he was here on the 18th or not.

538
00:38:21,199 --> 00:38:23,100
The 18th? Rings no bells.

539
00:38:23,234 --> 00:38:24,896
Wait a minute, the 18th?

540
00:38:25,036 --> 00:38:26,680
That was the week they were
tearing up the street out there.

541
00:38:26,704 --> 00:38:28,900
Oh, yeah, yeah, I remember now.

542
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:31,719
He was giving me a hard time because
he couldn't use the front entrance.

543
00:38:31,743 --> 00:38:35,202
He had to come around back, and I was
giving him a hard time about his car again.

544
00:38:35,346 --> 00:38:36,871
What about his car?

545
00:38:37,015 --> 00:38:39,610
He came in a cab. His car
is always breaking down.

546
00:38:39,751 --> 00:38:41,982
He drives a terrible
vehicle. I have a better one.

547
00:38:46,124 --> 00:38:51,028
Mr. Lerner, would you please
tell the court who Jeffrey Aiello is?

548
00:38:51,162 --> 00:38:52,824
I don't know any Jeffrey Aiello.

549
00:38:52,964 --> 00:38:54,023
No?

550
00:38:54,165 --> 00:38:57,101
According to the records we
subpoenaed from the phone company,

551
00:38:57,235 --> 00:38:59,329
he called your office
three times last month.

552
00:38:59,470 --> 00:39:01,564
Then he must have been
trying to sell me something

553
00:39:01,706 --> 00:39:03,402
because I sure
don't remember him.

554
00:39:03,541 --> 00:39:08,639
Jeffrey Aiello did time for passing
counterfeit bank checks a while back.

555
00:39:08,780 --> 00:39:09,941
Did you know that?

556
00:39:10,081 --> 00:39:11,447
Of course not.

557
00:39:11,583 --> 00:39:13,882
He wasn't trying to
sell you something.

558
00:39:14,018 --> 00:39:18,285
He was trying to buy a bunch of
counterfeit money from you, wasn't he?

559
00:39:18,423 --> 00:39:20,483
I don't know what
you're talking about.

560
00:39:20,625 --> 00:39:24,357
Only you and your friends got a
better deal from Charles Beckman,

561
00:39:24,495 --> 00:39:28,796
so you told Mr. Aiello to take a
hike... All except Ms. Westlake.

562
00:39:28,933 --> 00:39:31,434
She decided to print
up a few more bills

563
00:39:31,458 --> 00:39:34,236
and do business with
Mr. Aiello after all...

564
00:39:34,372 --> 00:39:38,332
Only secretly, so she
wouldn't have to cut in anybody.

565
00:39:38,476 --> 00:39:41,537
And that's what the fight was about
in the corridor yesterday, wasn't it?

566
00:39:41,679 --> 00:39:42,772
Oh, no, no, no.

567
00:39:42,914 --> 00:39:45,247
And that's what her
meeting with Mr. Aiello was

568
00:39:45,383 --> 00:39:47,045
at the time of the
murder, wasn't it?

569
00:39:47,185 --> 00:39:49,848
How should I know
what Caroline was doing?

570
00:39:49,988 --> 00:39:54,153
You know, the funny thing is
when she double-crossed you,

571
00:39:54,292 --> 00:39:57,387
she gave herself an alibi
for the time of the murder.

572
00:39:57,528 --> 00:39:59,156
And she has one,
but you don't, do you?

573
00:39:59,297 --> 00:40:02,495
I was at Chez Sal's
with a prospective client.

574
00:40:02,634 --> 00:40:04,967
Is that how you got
those cuts on your arm?

575
00:40:06,304 --> 00:40:08,000
Would you mind
pushing up your sleeve?

576
00:40:09,173 --> 00:40:10,641
Push it up. Push it up.

577
00:40:12,610 --> 00:40:16,706
These are not cuts, they're
scratches. I got them pruning my roses.

578
00:40:16,848 --> 00:40:18,783
They didn't come from roses.

579
00:40:18,917 --> 00:40:22,410
They came from this car.

580
00:40:22,553 --> 00:40:24,351
Diana Huntington's car.

581
00:40:24,489 --> 00:40:26,321
You stole it and used
it to kill her husband.

582
00:40:26,457 --> 00:40:28,323
That's not true.

583
00:40:28,459 --> 00:40:31,019
But you sideswiped a
post during the killing,

584
00:40:31,162 --> 00:40:33,631
and the window on the
driver's side shattered,

585
00:40:33,765 --> 00:40:38,032
and glass came flying through, all
over your arm and, look, even up here.

586
00:40:38,169 --> 00:40:39,933
And that's how
you got those cuts.

587
00:40:40,071 --> 00:40:42,563
Objection. This is all
speculation, Your Honor.

588
00:40:42,707 --> 00:40:44,505
And where is it leading?

589
00:40:44,642 --> 00:40:47,544
Mr. Matlock, you have
a knack for storytelling.

590
00:40:47,679 --> 00:40:48,806
Where is it leading?

591
00:40:51,549 --> 00:40:54,849
It's leading to reasonable
doubt, Your Honor.

592
00:40:54,986 --> 00:40:58,582
Reasonable doubt that
my client killed her husband.

593
00:41:00,325 --> 00:41:02,885
- Proceed.
- Thank you.

594
00:41:04,062 --> 00:41:06,725
Now we were talking about
where you got those cuts.

595
00:41:06,864 --> 00:41:11,802
I was at a business meeting at Chez
Sal's that morning from 8 till almost 9:30.

596
00:41:11,936 --> 00:41:13,768
You got those cuts
in a restaurant?

597
00:41:13,905 --> 00:41:15,396
No, from my roses.

598
00:41:15,540 --> 00:41:16,540
Oh.

599
00:41:16,607 --> 00:41:19,873
How did you get
to that restaurant?

600
00:41:20,011 --> 00:41:21,411
I drove.

601
00:41:21,546 --> 00:41:24,106
You sure you didn't come by cab?

602
00:41:24,248 --> 00:41:25,739
Yes.

603
00:41:25,883 --> 00:41:31,982
Well, the doorman at
Chez Sal's said you did.

604
00:41:33,958 --> 00:41:37,326
He's right. My car broke
down. I called a cab.

605
00:41:37,462 --> 00:41:38,953
I was mistaken.

606
00:41:39,097 --> 00:41:41,157
So you were late
for your meeting?

607
00:41:41,299 --> 00:41:43,325
- A little, yes.
- Yeah.

608
00:41:43,468 --> 00:41:48,429
The Red and White Cab Company says they
didn't pick you up till well after 8:30.

609
00:41:48,573 --> 00:41:52,032
So you were not at the
restaurant at the time of the murder.

610
00:41:52,176 --> 00:41:54,202
I was trying to get there.

611
00:41:54,345 --> 00:41:56,644
They said they picked
you up at a gas station.

612
00:41:56,781 --> 00:41:59,683
Yes. I had my car towed
there so they could work on it.

613
00:41:59,817 --> 00:42:01,877
- Towed from where?
- My house.

614
00:42:02,020 --> 00:42:04,216
- Who towed it?
- I don't remember.

615
00:42:04,355 --> 00:42:06,620
Wally's Garage.

616
00:42:06,758 --> 00:42:08,920
I have the work
order right here.

617
00:42:10,294 --> 00:42:11,353
See?

618
00:42:13,498 --> 00:42:17,435
You paid cash and
gave a phony name.

619
00:42:17,568 --> 00:42:19,036
Why did you do that, Mr. Lerner?

620
00:42:19,170 --> 00:42:21,765
I didn't. Obviously you've
got the wrong work order.

621
00:42:21,906 --> 00:42:24,273
Well, this is your license
plate number, isn't it?

622
00:42:24,409 --> 00:42:25,775
They wrote it down.

623
00:42:25,910 --> 00:42:29,938
They also wrote down the location
of your car when they picked it up.

624
00:42:30,081 --> 00:42:35,042
See? 17000 block of Cortez
Avenue. Is that where you live?

625
00:42:35,186 --> 00:42:37,246
There must be some mistake.

626
00:42:37,388 --> 00:42:39,619
No. No. You know where that is?

627
00:42:39,757 --> 00:42:42,192
It's right around the corner
from Diana Huntington's house.

628
00:42:42,326 --> 00:42:46,696
You parked there,
walked up to her house,

629
00:42:46,831 --> 00:42:48,697
stole her car from her garage,

630
00:42:48,833 --> 00:42:52,827
used it to kill her
husband, then put it back.

631
00:42:52,970 --> 00:42:56,907
Then walked around
the corner to you car.

632
00:42:57,041 --> 00:43:01,308
It wouldn't start, so
you used your car phone

633
00:43:01,446 --> 00:43:05,281
and called Wally's Garage,
the closest one you could find,

634
00:43:05,416 --> 00:43:07,681
and they towed you
to that gas station.

635
00:43:07,819 --> 00:43:10,914
And from there you went by
cab to the restaurant, didn't you?

636
00:43:11,055 --> 00:43:15,151
All because Jack Huntington
walked in on that meeting

637
00:43:15,293 --> 00:43:19,731
and inadvertently saw
that sheet of counterfeit 50s.

638
00:43:19,864 --> 00:43:22,095
He'd already been making waves,

639
00:43:22,233 --> 00:43:25,567
asking questions about that
photograph the three of you fabricated

640
00:43:25,703 --> 00:43:30,368
to give John Resnick an
alibi for Hugo Wykoff's murder.

641
00:43:30,508 --> 00:43:36,675
Then sometime later, for whatever your
reason, one of you killed John Resnick.

642
00:43:36,814 --> 00:43:38,715
Maybe it was you, Mr. Lerner.

643
00:43:39,851 --> 00:43:42,446
It was one of you,
that I'm sure of.

644
00:43:42,587 --> 00:43:46,456
And pretty soon the police
will figure out which one.

645
00:43:46,591 --> 00:43:50,551
Counterfeiting is against
the law, Mr. Lerner,

646
00:43:50,695 --> 00:43:57,727
but killing three people... And
one of them... Jack Huntington...

647
00:43:57,869 --> 00:44:02,967
Just in your way,
kind of like a fly.

648
00:44:11,749 --> 00:44:13,217
Nothing further.

649
00:44:18,623 --> 00:44:22,526
We find the defendant,
Diana Huntington, not guilty.

650
00:44:23,561 --> 00:44:25,723
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury.

651
00:44:25,863 --> 00:44:28,162
Thank you one and
all. Court is adjourned.

652
00:44:32,970 --> 00:44:34,632
Diana, Diana, Diana.

653
00:44:34,772 --> 00:44:36,412
I'm glad everything
turned out all right.

654
00:44:36,507 --> 00:44:37,507
Thank you.

655
00:44:37,575 --> 00:44:40,773
Listen, I promised you that I
would get into therapy, and I will.

656
00:44:40,912 --> 00:44:43,507
I never go back on my
bargains, even when I'm mad.

657
00:44:43,648 --> 00:44:44,809
Good. Good. Great.

658
00:44:44,949 --> 00:44:46,781
- Would you do me a favor?
- No.

659
00:44:46,918 --> 00:44:48,546
I beg your pardon?

660
00:44:48,686 --> 00:44:52,521
Doing you a favor is what got
us into this mess in the first place.

661
00:44:52,657 --> 00:44:57,618
I should have said no the first time,
and it's this time definitely no. No.

662
00:44:59,864 --> 00:45:02,857
Well, I was going to ask
you to loan me your pen

663
00:45:03,000 --> 00:45:04,559
so I could write a
check for your fee,

664
00:45:04,702 --> 00:45:07,297
but if that's the way you
feel, then the hell with you.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
